WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.478
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C one, Book three.

2
00:00:05.778 --> 00:00:09.826
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:10.126 --> 00:00:17.399
Unit twenty-five: Decolonisation. Today you will sequence historical events with perfect participle clauses.

4
00:00:17.699 --> 00:00:20.282
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:21.233 --> 00:00:24.246
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:25.083 --> 00:00:26.479
Decolonisation.

7
00:00:27.483 --> 00:00:31.375
Decolonisation means the process of colonies becoming independent.

8
00:00:31.983 --> 00:00:35.597
Decolonisation accelerated after 1945.

9
00:00:36.183 --> 00:00:38.685
Your turn. Say: Decolonisation.

10
00:00:41.533 --> 00:00:42.848
Decolonisation.

11
00:00:43.583 --> 00:00:44.422
Colony.

12
00:00:45.417 --> 00:00:48.473
Colony means a territory controlled by another country.

13
00:00:49.083 --> 00:00:51.559
Cambodia was a French colony in practice.

14
00:00:52.150 --> 00:00:54.268
Your turn. Say: Colony.

15
00:00:57.133 --> 00:00:58.065
Colony.

16
00:00:58.817 --> 00:01:00.038
Independence.

17
00:01:01.050 --> 00:01:04.768
Independence means self-government free from foreign control.

18
00:01:05.383 --> 00:01:07.894
Independence brought new responsibilities.

19
00:01:08.483 --> 00:01:11.426
Your turn. Say: Independence.

20
00:01:14.267 --> 00:01:15.500
Independence.

21
00:01:16.250 --> 00:01:17.321
Empire.

22
00:01:18.317 --> 00:01:21.582
Empire means a group of territories ruled by one power.

23
00:01:22.183 --> 00:01:24.938
The British empire was the largest in history.

24
00:01:25.550 --> 00:01:28.330
Your turn. Say: Empire.

25
00:01:31.167 --> 00:01:32.237
Empire.

26
00:01:32.983 --> 00:01:34.449
Self-determination.

27
00:01:35.450 --> 00:01:38.994
Self-determination means a people's right to choose their own government.

28
00:01:39.583 --> 00:01:42.175
The UN Charter recognised self-determination.

29
00:01:42.783 --> 00:01:45.714
Your turn. Say: Self-determination.

30
00:01:48.567 --> 00:01:50.148
Self-determination.

31
00:01:50.883 --> 00:01:51.861
Legacy.

32
00:01:52.850 --> 00:01:55.732
Legacy means something left behind from the past.

33
00:01:56.317 --> 00:01:59.291
Colonial legacies shaped new constitutions.

34
00:01:59.883 --> 00:02:02.269
Your turn. Say: Legacy.

35
00:02:05.133 --> 00:02:06.285
Legacy.

36
00:02:07.050 --> 00:02:08.446
Nation-building.

37
00:02:09.450 --> 00:02:13.528
Nation-building means creating institutions and identity for a new state.

38
00:02:14.117 --> 00:02:16.848
Nation-building required schools and roads.

39
00:02:17.450 --> 00:02:20.033
Your turn. Say: Nation-building.

40
00:02:22.867 --> 00:02:24.913
Nation-building.

41
00:02:25.650 --> 00:02:26.779
Sovereignty.

42
00:02:27.783 --> 00:02:31.281
Sovereignty means supreme authority over one's own territory.

43
00:02:31.883 --> 00:02:35.090
Sovereignty was restored in 1953.

44
00:02:35.683 --> 00:02:38.208
Your turn. Say: Sovereignty.

45
00:02:41.067 --> 00:02:42.254
Sovereignty.

46
00:02:43.067 --> 00:02:46.694
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:47.550 --> 00:02:53.230
Visal, what happened after Cambodia gained independence in 1953?

48
00:02:54.317 --> 00:03:05.987
Having gained independence, the new government began nation-building: building schools, universities and a national identity.

49
00:03:07.083 --> 00:03:08.804
Was it an easy transition?

50
00:03:09.917 --> 00:03:20.348
Far from it. Having inherited borders and institutions designed for colonial rule, many new states struggled to govern effectively.

51
00:03:21.450 --> 00:03:22.857
And internationally?

52
00:03:23.950 --> 00:03:33.647
Having been drawn into the Cold War, several former colonies faced pressure from both superpowers, which made genuine sovereignty difficult.

53
00:03:34.800 --> 00:03:36.741
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:37.583 --> 00:03:40.663
Grammar focus: Participle clauses of time.

55
00:03:40.963 --> 00:03:42.149
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:42.850 --> 00:03:46.220
Having gained independence, many nations began nation-building.

57
00:03:47.117 --> 00:03:51.589
Having inherited colonial institutions, new states struggled to govern.

58
00:03:52.483 --> 00:03:56.468
Having been drawn into the Cold War, former colonies faced pressure.

59
00:03:57.433 --> 00:04:09.855
'Having + p.p.' shows that one action was completed before the next; 'Having been + p.p.' is the passive form. The subject of both clauses must be the same: 'Having gained independence, the nation…'.

60
00:04:10.617 --> 00:04:14.163
Be careful! Don't say: Having gain independence, …

61
00:04:16.100 --> 00:04:18.750
Say: Having gained independence, …

62
00:04:19.050 --> 00:04:20.933
Having + past participle.

63
00:04:21.733 --> 00:04:24.200
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:25.000 --> 00:04:26.419
The End of Empires.

65
00:04:26.719 --> 00:04:34.257
Between 1945 and 1975, more than eighty former colonies became independent states.

66
00:04:35.267 --> 00:04:41.620
Having been weakened by the Second World War, European powers found it increasingly difficult to maintain their empires.

67
00:04:42.633 --> 00:04:49.091
Some territories gained independence through negotiation, while others achieved it only after long and violent wars.

68
00:04:50.100 --> 00:04:56.337
Having won sovereignty, new governments faced the enormous challenge of building national institutions and economies.

69
00:04:57.333 --> 00:05:03.431
Decolonisation thus transformed the map of the world, but its economic and political legacies remain contested.

70
00:05:04.533 --> 00:05:08.454
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:09.317 --> 00:05:12.466
blank independence, the country wrote a constitution.

72
00:05:12.766 --> 00:05:16.524
A, Having gained. B, Having gain. C, Have gained.

73
00:05:20.067 --> 00:05:22.159
The answer is A: Having gained.

74
00:05:23.317 --> 00:05:25.943
blank by the war, the empire could not resist.

75
00:05:26.243 --> 00:05:30.721
A, Having weakened. B, Having been weakened. C, Been having weakened.

76
00:05:34.267 --> 00:05:36.823
The answer is B: Having been weakened.

77
00:05:37.983 --> 00:05:40.529
A people's right to choose their government is blank.

78
00:05:40.829 --> 00:05:44.912
A, self-determination. B, colony. C, legacy.

79
00:05:48.467 --> 00:05:50.861
The answer is A: self-determination.

80
00:05:52.017 --> 00:05:55.781
Creating institutions and identity for a new state is blank.

81
00:05:56.081 --> 00:06:00.443
A, decolonisation. B, nation-building. C, empire.

82
00:06:04.000 --> 00:06:06.650
The answer is B: nation-building.

83
00:06:07.783 --> 00:06:15.843
According to the reading, how many colonies became independent between 1945 and 1975?

84
00:06:16.143 --> 00:06:20.238
A, about twenty. B, more than eighty. C, fewer than ten.

85
00:06:23.800 --> 00:06:25.776
The answer is B: more than eighty.

86
00:06:26.967 --> 00:06:30.107
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:30.950 --> 00:06:34.134
What challenges did newly independent nations face?

88
00:06:50.950 --> 00:06:52.520
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:52.820 --> 00:07:28.778
Newly independent nations faced enormous challenges. Having inherited borders and institutions designed for colonial rule, many governments struggled to unite diverse populations and manage weak economies. They also lacked trained teachers, doctors and administrators, because colonial powers had invested little in local education. In addition, the Cold War drew many of them into conflicts they had not chosen, as happened in Cambodia. Their experience shows that independence was only the beginning.

90
00:07:29.078 --> 00:07:30.694
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:31.583 --> 00:07:35.989
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and participle clauses of time.

92
00:07:36.289 --> 00:07:40.915
Today's value is perseverance. Keep trying, even when it is hard.

93
00:07:41.215 --> 00:07:45.086
See you in Unit twenty-six. Goodbye!

94
00:07:45.386 --> 00:07:49.107
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

